  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799006320: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 6320 Subsistence Livestock Farmers farm record-keeping/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 6320\n-- Subsistence Livestock Farmers** only, part of Wave3\n(physical-production, `kotoba.occupation.wave` sub-major \"63\").\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"6320\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"6320\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Subsistence Livestock Farmers\" --\nconfirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository\nexisted at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6320` (`gh api` 404\nconfirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry\nedit promote the `6320` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries a livelihood-vulnerability dimension distinct\nfrom this fleet's other transport/logistics actors: subsistence\nlivestock farmers raise animals primarily for household consumption,\nso livestock loss directly threatens household food security, on top\nof the ordinary animal-welfare dimension and standard animal-handling\nphysical-safety hazards.** A wrongful automated decision could\ncompound harm across multiple axes at once -- a treatment/welfare\nmisjudgment harming the animal, or a livelihood-affecting decision made\nwithout the farmer's own consent. The controlling precedent is\n**ADR-2607152500** (Wave4 rollout start amendment), which established\nthat decisions directly touching safety, dignity or subsistence must\nalways exclude the closed op allowlist and always escalate to a human,\nnot merely be gated behind a confidence threshold. This ADR applies\nthat guardrail shape here: no op anywhere in this actor's closed\nallowlist may finalize an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision,\nor override the farmer's own judgment about their household's\nlivestock.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and for the\ndual-safety-dimension guardrail pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332)\n(Drivers of Animal-drawn Vehicles and Machinery: `cartage.store`/\n`cartage.advisor`/`cartage.governor`/`cartage.actor` split,\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, closed-allowlist +\nregistered-AND-verified provenance + content-based scope-exclusion\nsafety pattern), cloned and read in full this session, along with\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111` for the standard module shape.\nThe road-safety half of 9332's dual-dimension guardrail does not apply\nto this vertical (no vehicle, no traffic navigation); it is replaced\nhere by the livelihood-vulnerability dimension unique to subsistence\nfarming.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6320` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for subsistence livestock\nfarmers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Farm Operations Advisor\n⊣ HusbandryGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different library coordinate from\nthe `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **farm record-keeping/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never animal-handling or treatment authority** --\nit must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising ANY of: an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an\noverride of the farmer's own judgment about their household's\nlivestock. These are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are\nstructurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely, and enforced a\nsecond, independent way via a content-based scope-exclusion check over\nfree text.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in\n  data logging (metadata only -- work id, feeding schedule, condition\n  check-in, timestamp -- NEVER a treatment decision, a welfare\n  disposition, or a breeding decision)\n- `:schedule-farm-operation` -- household labor/task scheduling\n  proposal (advance scheduling logistics only -- never a treatment\n  directive or a real-time farmer-judgment override)\n- `:flag-welfare-concern` -- surface an animal-welfare/injury-risk/\n  livelihood-loss concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- feed/supplies procurement proposal,\n  escalates above a registered per-holding cost threshold\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**: an\nanimal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an override of the\nfarmer's own judgment about their household's livestock. These are\n**structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely\ngated behind escalation -- an op such as\n`:determine-animal-fitness-for-breeding` or\n`:authorize-veterinary-treatment` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of\nthe four allowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no default\nbranch producing any other op shape), and if any code path (a forged\nproposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such\nan op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a)\nunit tests directly against `governor/check` for twelve\nconcretely-named forbidden op fixtures\n(`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:determine-animal-fitness-for-breeding`,\n`:decide-animal-fitness-for-breeding`,\n`:authorize-veterinary-treatment`, `:order-veterinary-treatment`,\n`:administer-veterinary-treatment`,\n`:decide-animal-welfare-disposition`,\n`:determine-animal-welfare-disposition`, `:finalize-breeding-decision`,\n`:determine-breeding-decision`, `:authorize-livestock-cull`,\n`:order-livestock-cull`, `:override-farmer-livestock-judgment`,\n`:override-household-livestock-judgment`), and (b) an END-TO-END test\nthat drives each of those same twelve ops through a ROGUE advisor and\nthe FULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure\ncheck function), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to\n`:hold` with zero records committed even when the advisor node itself\nis compromised\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Seven HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Farmer unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently `:verified?` farmer/livestock-holding record.\n   Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the farmer is.\n   Directly implements the task's \"farmer/livestock-holding record\n   must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n   invariant for the farmer half.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance -- the\n   governor never dispatches hardware, never handles the animals\n   directly).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in a breeding-fitness/veterinary-treatment/welfare-\n   disposition/farmer-judgment-override-shaped op.\n4. **Holding basis** -- `:log-work-record`, `:schedule-farm-operation`\n   and `:coordinate-supply-order` must cite a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED\n   holding matching the farmer's own holding (`:missing-holding-id` /\n   `:unknown-holding` / `:holding-unverified` / `:holding-mismatch`).\n   `:flag-welfare-concern` is deliberately EXEMPT from this check -- it\n   is the channel by which a brand-new holding, or an urgent welfare/\n   livelihood-loss concern with no holding on file yet, is surfaced\n   for human intake, so it must not be blocked on the very holding\n   record it exists to help flag.\n5. **Work-record decision forbidden** -- `:log-work-record` is a\n   feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in metadata record ONLY.\n   Any proposal carrying an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding-decision\n   field (`husbandry.governor/log-record-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:treatment-decision`, `:veterinary-treatment-order`,\n   `:welfare-disposition`, `:breeding-decision`,\n   `:breeding-fitness-decision`, `:cull-decision`) is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block -- this actor never records a treatment decision, a welfare\n   disposition, or a breeding decision, only metadata check-ins.\n6. **Farm-schedule override forbidden** -- `:schedule-farm-operation`\n   is household labor/task scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal\n   carrying a treatment-directive or farmer-judgment-override field\n   (`husbandry.governor/schedule-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:treatment-directive`, `:farmer-judgment-override`,\n   `:livestock-judgment-override`, `:breeding-directive`,\n   `:cull-directive`, `:welfare-disposition`) is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block -- this actor never overrides the farmer's own judgment about\n   their household's livestock, it only schedules which household\n   member/task is assigned to which farm-operation slot in advance.\n7. **Scope exclusion** -- independent, defense-in-depth layer on top of\n   checks 3/5/6: even for an otherwise-allowed op, any proposal whose\n   free text (`:rationale` or `:note`) names a finalization/execution\n   ACTION for an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an\n   override of the farmer's own judgment about their household's\n   livestock (`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`, e.g.\n   \"determine the animal's fitness for breeding\", \"order the\n   veterinary treatment\", \"override the farmer's livestock judgment\",\n   plus Japanese equivalents) is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content.\n\n### Two escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-welfare-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting an\n  animal-welfare, injury-risk, or livelihood-loss concern needs human\n  attention -- it never resolves the observation itself, and the\n  robot's role ends at \"here is the feeding/roster/condition-check-in\n  status,\" never \"here is whether the animal needs treatment\" or\n  \"here is whether the animal should be bred.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (300, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`; set lower than\n  9332's 800 maintenance-cost threshold because subsistence-livestock\n  households operate on tighter margins than a commercial dispatch\n  depot) -- always needs human sign-off, independent of confidence.\n  This is an ESCALATION, not a hard block: an over-budget supply order\n  (e.g. a full fencing replacement) is not itself unsafe, unlike an\n  animal-treatment/welfare/breeding, or farmer-judgment-override\n  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft\n  escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"breeding\", \"welfare\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path. This risk is especially\nsharp here because this actor's own op is literally named\n`:flag-welfare-concern` -- a bare \"welfare\" term would self-trip on\nevery single legitimate welfare-flag proposal.\n`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"determine the animal's fitness\nfor breeding\", \"override the farmer's livestock judgment\" -- the exact\nphrasing pattern given in the task's guidance on this known bug\nclass), never bare nouns, and\n`husbandry.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-\nscope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting every op in\nthe closed allowlist (including both the holding-present and\nholding-nil variants of `:flag-welfare-concern`), driven through the\ndefault mock advisor, never trips `:scope-excluded` or\n`:op-not-allowed`. The mock advisor's own default rationale template\n(\"documented <op> for holding <id>\" / \"documented <op> (no holding\nyet -- new-holding intake)\") was written to avoid any bare-noun\ncollision by construction. Green on first run this session -- the\nscope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during\ndevelopment or verification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"6320\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6320` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-6320`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  retry batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 44 tests, 152 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No implementation bug was found during this session's verification\n  pass -- the test suite was green on first run against both the local\n  working tree and the fresh post-push clone.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-9332`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-treatment-decision`/\n  `:approve-breeding-decision` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops` (the shape a naive reading of \"gate high-stakes\n  actions behind human approval\" might suggest): rejected outright per\n  the task's explicit guidance and the `ADR-2607152500` precedent -- an\n  always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  treatment or breeding decision exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. For decisions carrying both an\n  animal-welfare and a household-livelihood dimension, the capability\n  itself must not exist. The only op related to escalating a concern is\n  `:flag-welfare-concern`, which surfaces the need for human attention\n  and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a treatment/breeding\n  action for later automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"breeding\" \"welfare\"\n  \"treatment\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above -- this actor's own op is literally named\n  `:flag-welfare-concern`, so a bare \"welfare\" term would self-trip on\n  every legitimate welfare-flag proposal.\n- Merging `farmer` and `holding` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the holding-mismatch check\n  requires two independently registered-AND-verified entities so that\n  a holding record a farmer is not assigned to is caught even if both\n  records individually pass verification.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-work-record` /\n  `:schedule-farm-operation` (no forbidden-keys content checks):\n  rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that\n  proposal smuggles forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could\n  still misuse an allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden\n  treatment or breeding decision, which is exactly why checks 5/6 exist\n  as independent, content-based, permanent HARD blocks regardless of\n  confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in\n   metadata only (work id, feeding schedule, condition check-in,\n   timestamp). Cannot carry a treatment, welfare, or breeding decision\n   (HARD-blocked by check 5, `:work-record-decision-forbidden`, on a\n   closed forbidden-key list). Not an animal-treatment/welfare/\n   breeding decision in any respect -- it is a records-management\n   action indistinguishable in kind from a farm clerk's log entry.\n2. **`:schedule-farm-operation`** -- household labor/task scheduling\n   logistics only (task id, proposed time, assignee). Cannot carry a\n   treatment directive or a real-time farmer-judgment override\n   (HARD-blocked by check 6, `:farm-schedule-override-forbidden`, on a\n   closed forbidden-key list). Does not direct, override, or draw any\n   conclusion about a live treatment or breeding decision -- it is a\n   calendar action deciding in advance which household member/task is\n   assigned to which farm-operation slot.\n3. **`:flag-welfare-concern`** -- surfaces an animal-welfare/\n   injury-risk/livelihood-loss concern to a human; ALWAYS escalates\n   (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which such a concern may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- feed/supplies procurement proposal\n   (item, cost, vendor). Gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation\n   (above 300 always escalates). Not an animal-welfare or breeding\n   action of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action for feed/\n   supplies upkeep.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\nan animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an override of the\nfarmer's own judgment about their household's livestock -- these\ncategories are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the\nclosed four-op allowlist itself (`husbandry.governor/closed-op-\nallowlist`), (b) twelve concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures that are\nrejected at both the governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph level\nvia a rogue advisor (`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`husbandry.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`husbandry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`husbandry.governor/out-of-scope?`,\n`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), tested end-to-end through a\nrogue advisor and the full compiled graph\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-rationale-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its own\nfield shape (checks 5/6 above), both independently proven end-to-end\nvia a rogue advisor driving the full compiled StateGraph\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-treatment-decision-attempt`,\n`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-farm-schedule-override-attempt`).\n")
  (:adr/date "2026-07-18")
  (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 300}")
  (:test/counts "{:total 44, :assertions 152, :failures 0, :errors 0}")
  (:actor/coordination? true)
  (:actor/name "Subsistence Livestock Farm Record-Keeping and Logistics Coordination Practice")
  (:db/id -1)
  (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract
                   :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :farmer-verification :holding-verification
                   :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :work-record-decision-forbidden
                   :farm-schedule-override-forbidden :rogue-advisor-end-to-end))
